WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.540
INTRO:
Over the past couple of weeks, I posted a

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:06.780
couple of videos discussing the whereabouts
of popular piracy website founders including

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.320
MegaUpload, KickassTorrents, BitTorrent, and
of course The Pirate Bay.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:16.209
In each and every one of these videos, I noticed
a few common sets of comments.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:18.560
First, you have the nostalgia-filled comments.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.430
“I was like pirate bay??

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:21.430
Oooooh yeeaaa.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:22.430
The nostalgia.”

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:26.440
“Duuude you gave me real nostalgia when
you mentioned Isohunt.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.640
That used to be the BOMB when TPB was taken
down.”

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.910
And then, there are the comments that claim
that these founders are heroes.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.840
“These guys are legends.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.800
They provided access to information that was
not available to people who couldn't afford

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:39.800
it.”

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.550
“In the world without copyrights, we could've
been heroes said the Pirate Bay creators”.

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:47.429
After that, you have the comments that are
like, I used to pirate because I was either

00:00:47.429 --> 00:00:49.440
a kid or broke or both.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.199
But, now that I can afford it, I support content
creators.

00:00:53.199 --> 00:00:58.069
And finally, you have the ones that have no
remorse and outrightly support piracy and

00:00:58.069 --> 00:01:01.040
think that the entire concept of copyright
is wrong.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.049
“Just a friendly reminder: It's always morally
accurate to use pirated Adobe”.

00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:11.079
“Love how piracy is referred to as immoral,
yet more than 80% of companies and services

00:01:11.079 --> 00:01:15.020
wouldn't know morality if it hit them in the
face, double standard?”

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.500
Clearly, people seem to be quite passionate
and divided on this topic.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.060
Either they pirate due to their situation
or they pirate because they couldn’t care

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.640
less if some big corporation makes less money.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.710
So, is piracy morally wrong or just merely
illegal?

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:36.430
MORALLY WRONG:
Starting with one of the most popular arguments

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:39.609
in support of piracy, we have the broke argument.

00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:43.259
Someone doesn’t have any money, so they
feel that if they want to enjoy a piece of

00:01:43.259 --> 00:01:45.950
content or a game, they have to pirate it.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:50.710
Now, there is some argument to be made that
a lot of content is inaccessible to people

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.469
from developing countries.

00:01:52.469 --> 00:01:57.329
For example, a $60 game costs 1200 pesos or
4500 rupees.

00:01:57.329 --> 00:02:01.429
While these are technically equivalent amounts,
the cost of living and average incomes of

00:02:01.429 --> 00:02:03.469
these countries are radically different.

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:08.720
The average American household pulls in $67,000
per year while the average Indian household

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.150
only pulls in just 204,000 rupees or about
$2700 per year.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:16.330
Considering this, there’s no doubt that
the average person living in a developing

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:21.670
country simply cannot afford a lot of western
content whether that be games, movies, or

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:22.670
music.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:25.780
But, does this make piracy moral in these
situations?

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:31.120
Well, the basis of this argument is that affordability
is what determines morality.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.190
But that doesn’t make sense, there are plenty
of things that most people can’t afford.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:41.069
For example, I genuinely cannot afford G Wagon,
but I really want one.

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:43.040
Would it be ok for me to steal a G Wagon?

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.890
I don’t have any bad intentions, I hope
Mercedes does great and continues to make

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.160
phenomenal cars.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:52.500
I simply can’t afford it, and that’s why
I steal it.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.989
Clearly, there’s no doubt that stealing
the car would be morally wrong, so morality

00:02:56.989 --> 00:02:59.220
is not based on affordability.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.849
Hearing this, a lot of pirates would argue
that by stealing that car, I’m directly

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:08.239
costing Mercedes money, but by pirating content,
they’re not costing Adobe or Microsoft any

00:03:08.239 --> 00:03:09.239
money.

00:03:09.239 --> 00:03:13.439
If anything, they’re simply losing potential
revenue, and many pirates argue that if they

00:03:13.439 --> 00:03:17.430
couldn’t pirate it, they wouldn’t have
paid for the content anyway, so there’s

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.409
no loss in revenue either.

00:03:19.409 --> 00:03:23.330
In other words, they’re arguing that morality
is dependent on whether there’s financial

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:24.520
consequences.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:27.939
First of all, I think there’s a fundamental
flaw with this argument.

00:03:27.939 --> 00:03:31.299
If you truly weren’t interested in a piece
of content, you wouldn’t have gone through

00:03:31.299 --> 00:03:33.060
the hassle of pirating.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.989
Usually, when people make this argument, I
think what they’re trying to say is that

00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:40.879
they wouldn’t have paid full price for the
content because they don’t think it’s

00:03:40.879 --> 00:03:45.790
worth that much, so clearly, there’s some
sort of loss in revenue.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:50.540
But is it even up to you to decide whether
a piece of content deserves money or not?

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:54.140
The basis of this argument is essentially
that the morality of piracy depends on the

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:55.800
quality of the content.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.750
If a piece of content is worth the money,
it’s not ok to pirate.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:04.401
But if a piece of content is garbage, pirate
all you want, but this doesn’t really make

00:04:04.401 --> 00:04:05.401
sense either.

00:04:05.401 --> 00:04:09.689
Let’s say you go to a theatre to watch a
movie, and it was absolute trash.

00:04:09.689 --> 00:04:12.670
You wouldn’t watch that movie even if you
got it for free.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:15.629
In this case, is it moral to ask for a refund?

00:04:15.629 --> 00:04:19.780
The theatre didn’t spend any extra money
to show the film to you specifically.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:23.210
Even if you didn’t go, the film would’ve
played and there would’ve likely been a

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:24.440
lot of empty seats.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:29.330
So, by asking for a refund, you’re not costing
the theater any money, they’re just losing

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:30.500
potential revenue.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:34.990
But, what if everyone else who watched the
movie hated it just as much as you.

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:37.920
Is it moral if you get a refund and all of
them didn’t?

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.550
Clearly, that would be unfair.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:43.410
Either everyone should get a refund or no
one should.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:48.360
So, morality doesn’t depend on affordability,
financial consequences, or the quality of

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:49.360
the content.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.470
In other words, if you’re pirating a piece
of content because you either can’t or don’t

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:56.970
want to pay for it, I’d argue that it’s
probably still immoral.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:01.380
But, there are cases in which it’s not so
simple.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:06.190
MORALLY DICEY:
One of the most understandable arguments within

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:09.370
the piracy scene is pirating for professional
development.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:12.360
Maybe you’re a freelance architect or a
photo editor.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.270
In such cases, you could use free software,
but nothing quite compares to Autodesk Inventor

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:18.970
or Adobe Photoshop.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:22.990
And when everyone else that you’re competing
against is using this software, it’s extremely

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:26.770
difficult for you to produce a similar quality
of product using free software.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:31.620
So, by pirating professional software, you’re
simply evening out the playing field right?

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:36.280
You’re not trying to freeload or get something
that you don’t need, you’re just trying

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:40.030
to get the tools that are required to progress
in your career and provide for your family

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:41.710
and whatever else.

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:46.190
While these are definitely virtuous goals,
there is an argument that could be made against

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:47.190
this.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:50.680
Here’s the thing, professional software
is specifically designed to give professionals

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.150
a competitive advantage against the competition.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:56.940
So, is it fair for you to reap those same
benefits without paying?

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:01.330
Are they idiots for paying or are you just
a genius for getting it for free?

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:05.440
In my opinion, the answer isn’t clear-cut
in this scenario because there’s some morality

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.450
to both sides of the argument.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:10.130
And what about more basic pieces of content.

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:13.910
What if instead of trying to pirate Premiere
Pro and Excel, a high school student from

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:18.010
a developing country was trying to pirate
a physics or chemistry textbook just so they

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:19.729
could get through the class.

00:06:19.729 --> 00:06:24.160
These guys are just trying to get basic education
to improve their chances in the real world.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.000
Also, it’s not like these guys have a choice.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:31.810
If you’re simply pirating a movie for enjoyment,
you’re doing it out of free will, and there

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:35.020
aren’t any consequences for not pirating
the movie.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:37.639
Worst case scenario, you’ll be bored for
a few hours.

00:06:37.639 --> 00:06:41.690
But, if you don’t pirate a textbook that
you can’t afford, you may end up failing

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:46.030
the class and pushing back graduation, or
worse you may end up dropping out.

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:50.860
So, if you’re pirating for education or
professional use cases, the morality is still

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:55.530
sometimes dicey, but I’d argue that it’s
magnitudes more acceptable than pirating for

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:57.139
entertainment.

00:06:57.139 --> 00:07:02.819
MORALLY ACCEPTABLE:
So far, we’ve mostly been trashing on piracy,

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:05.319
but it’s not always so bad.

00:07:05.319 --> 00:07:09.780
There’s actually a handful of cases where
I think piracy is completely acceptable.

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.590
For example, what if a piece of content is
region blocked.

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:17.539
Season 5 of Better Call Saul came out 2 years
ago in February of 2020.

00:07:17.539 --> 00:07:21.400
Yet, on Netflix, you can only watch the first
4 seasons.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:25.660
This isn’t to say that season 5 hasn’t
been launched on Netflix as many European

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:30.150
countries were able to watch season 5 on Netflix
as soon as it aired on TV.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.639
But American Netflix viewers are still waiting.

00:07:32.639 --> 00:07:36.460
It’s not like these viewers are trying to
freeload; they’re willing to pay for it.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:40.430
In fact, they are paying for it given that
they have a Netflix subscription.

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:44.210
At least in this scenario, Americans have
another way of watching this content if they

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:48.970
have cable TV, but in many cases, a piece
of content is just straight up banned in a

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:53.099
region and there are no legal ways to pay
for it and watch it.

00:07:53.099 --> 00:07:56.990
In such a scenario, I don’t think it’s
morally wrong to pirate this region-blocked

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:57.990
content.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:01.819
Another case in which piracy is acceptable
is dead online games.

00:08:01.819 --> 00:08:06.699
At some point, almost every online game will
be shut down due to a dwindling player base.

00:08:06.699 --> 00:08:07.970
Take Minecraft for example.

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:11.199
Right now, it’s not in any risk of being
shut down.

00:08:11.199 --> 00:08:13.590
But, what if it was shut down 10 years from
now?

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:17.520
Would it be morally wrong for current players
of the game to pirate a cracked version of

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.620
the game that runs off of third-party servers?

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:24.210
In this scenario, not only did you pay for
the game originally, but the game developer

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:28.270
themselves shut down the game because it was
no longer profitable or lucrative enough for

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:29.270
them.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:32.570
So, if you pirate a dead game, you’re not
really stealing from the game developer given

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:34.580
that they aren’t even selling the game.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:39.840
Also, it’s not like you’re unwilling to
pay but rather that there is no way to pay.

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:44.350
So, I think we’d agree that there’s nothing
morally wrong about pirating dead games.

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:49.700
But there is one more thing to consider that
makes the entire discussion a lot more complex.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:55.530
COMPANIES WANT YOU TO PIRATE:
Up until this point, all of our arguments

00:08:55.530 --> 00:09:00.100
have been rooted in the perspective of consumers
whether that be students, professionals, or

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:01.570
just everyday people.

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:05.800
But, what about the perspective of companies
as that surely plays an important role in

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:06.980
all this as well.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:10.270
For example, let’s go back to the G Wagon
example.

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:13.040
What if Mercedes did a giveaway of a G Wagon?

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.840
If I win the giveaway, then I didn’t steal
the car, but I still got it for free.

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:21.810
And there’s nothing morally wrong with this
situation given that Mercedes is consenting

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:24.580
to giveaway away the car probably for marketing
purposes.

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:29.340
Now, it’s not like companies want you to
pirate, but there are significantly different

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:30.670
levels of toleration.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:35.780
The movie industry, for example, has no toleration
because they have little to benefit from piracy.

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:40.030
If you get a move for free, it’s extremely
unlikely that you’ll also pay for it later

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:41.030
on.

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:45.710
But with something like Photoshop or PowerPoint,
you may use it for free for 2,3,4 years.

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:49.640
But, if you start making good money through
your career, there’s a strong likelihood

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:53.080
that you become a 10, 20, 30-year long customer.

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:57.640
And for these companies, letting users pirate
for a few years is often a great investment.

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:01.530
In fact, some companies would rather you pirate
their software for life as opposed to using

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:03.320
software from the competition.

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:21.300
Just listen to what Bill Gates thinks about
piracy.

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:45.100
Bill was laughing as he said that, but I think
he was also being dead serious.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:48.850
One of the keys to Microsoft’s dominance
within the operating system industry is that

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:51.780
most people would rather pirate Windows than
use Linux.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:56.570
And that wouldn’t be possible if Microsoft
didn’t make Windows reasonably easy to pirate.

00:10:56.570 --> 00:11:01.100
With that in mind, it would be less morally
wrong to pirate Windows than to pirate Endgame.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:05.460
Speaking of the company’s side, many pirates
feel that the morality of pirating also depends

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:07.020
on the status of the company.

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:11.190
If a company is super big and/or corrupt,
it’s ok to pirate from them.

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:15.400
But, it’s not ok to pirate from small game
or software developers.

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:19.570
While I would argue that it’s morally wrong
to pirate from either of them, it would definitely

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:24.130
be more morally wrong to pirate from the smaller
developer as your individual actions have

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:26.180
a much greater impact on them.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:32.080
THE PIRACY DEBACLE:
At the end of the day, there is no clear-cut

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:34.360
answer regarding the morality of piracy.

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:38.680
In terms of a general rule of thumb though,
I think the answer to morality comes down

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:40.130
to the purpose of piracy.

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:44.700
If you’re pirating purely for entertainment,
it’s almost always morally wrong.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:49.480
If you’re pirating for education or career
development, it’s morally ambiguous and

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:52.460
depends on the specific situation.

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:56.330
If you’re willing to pay, but can’t get
the content legally though, then I think it’s

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:57.510
morally acceptable to pirate.

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:02.000
The company’s stance on piracy along with
their own actions also factors into this,

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:06.020
but I think the purpose-based determination
is a pretty good rule to go by.

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:07.890
But that’s just what I think.

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:09.670
Where do you guys stand on the spectrum?

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:10.700
Comment that down below.

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:14.290
Also, drop a like if you’re a pirate.

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:18.020
And of course, consider checking out our international
channels to watch our videos in other languages

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:33.200
and consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

